JVMTI (Java Virtual Machine Tools Interface) is a part of the Java VM that provides a way to instrument classes and monitor Java application execution. Here are some code examples of class transformations using JVMTI in Android:

**Example 1: Using ` JVMTI::ClassTransformer` to add a method**

```java
public class MyClassTransformer implements JVMTI::ClassTransformer {
    @Override
    public void transform(JVMTI Env, ClassTransformation transformation) {
        // Get the original class
        ClassObject originalClass = transformation.getOriginal();

        // Get the method to add
        MethodObject originalMethod = originalClass.getMethod("originalMethod");

        // Create a new method
        MethodObject newMethod = Env.createMethod(originalClass, "newMethod", "()V");

        // Set the implementation of the new method
        Env.setMethodImplementation(newMethod, new Object() {
            public void newMethod() {
                Log.d("Example", "New method called!");
            }
        });

        // Add the new method to the class
        Env.addMethod(originalClass, newMethod);
    }
}
```

**Example 2: Using `JVMTI::ClassTransformer` to modify an existing method**

```java
public class MyClassTransformer implements JVMTI::ClassTransformer {
    @Override
    public void transform(JVMTI Env, ClassTransformation transformation) {
        // Get the original class
        ClassObject originalClass = transformation.getOriginal();

        // Get the method to modify
        MethodObject originalMethod = originalClass.getMethod("originalMethod");

        // Set the implementation of the modified method
        Env.setMethodImplementation(originalMethod, new Object() {
            public void originalMethod() {
                Log.d("Example", "Modified method called!");
            }
        });
    }
}
```

**Example 3: Using `JVMTI::ClassTransformer` to remove a method**

```java
public class MyClassTransformer implements JVMTI::ClassTransformer {
    @Override
    public void transform(JVMTI Env, ClassTransformation transformation) {
        // Get the original class
        ClassObject originalClass = transformation.getOriginal();

        // Get the method to remove
        MethodObject originalMethod = originalClass.getMethod("originalMethod");

        // Remove the method
        Env.removeMethod(originalClass, originalMethod);
    }
}
```

**Example 4: Using `JVMTI::ClassTransformer` to add a field**

```java
public class MyClassTransformer implements JVMTI::ClassTransformer {
    @Override
    public void transform(JVMTI Env, ClassTransformation transformation) {
        // Get the original class
        ClassObject originalClass = transformation.getOriginal();

        // Create a new field
        FieldObject newField = Env.createField(originalClass, "newField", "I");

        // Set the value of the new field
        Env.setField-original(originalClass, newField, 10);

        // Add the new field to the class
        Env.addField(originalClass, newField);
    }
