```

These examples demonstrate how to use JVMTI class transformations to instrument classes and modify their behavior in an Android application.

**Note:** To use JVMTI in an Android application, you need to register a JVMTI agent in your application. This can be done by adding the following code to your `AndroidManifest.xml` file:
```xml
<application ...>
    ...
    <meta-data
        android:name="com.github.jvmti.agent"
        android:value="true" />
</application>
```
You also need to implement a `JVMTI::Agent` that will receive callbacks from the JVMTI system. For example:
```java
public class MyAgent implements JVMTI::Agent {
    @Override
    public void agent_start(JVMTI Env) {
        // Initialize the agent
        Env.setEventHook(JVMTI::EVENT_CLASS_LOAD, this::onClassLoaded);
    }

    @Override
    public void onClassLoaded(JVMTI Env, JVMTI::ClassLoadEvent event) {
        // Get the loaded class
        ClassObject loadedClass = event.getLoadedClass();

        // Transform the class
        MyClassTransformer transformer = new MyClassTransformer();
        Env.addClassTransformer(loadedClass, transformer);
    }
}
```
